<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saintes</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Mediolanum Santonum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saintes is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente">Charente</a> in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charente-maritime">Charente-Maritime</a>. As Mediolanum Santonum it was the chief town of the Gallic Santones and the first capital of the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gallia-aquitania">Aquitania</a>, and it keeps the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arch-of-germanicus">Arch of Germanicus</a> and a large amphitheatre from that period. It later became the capital of the province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saintonge">Saintonge</a>, and Louis IX defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-iii-of-england">Henry III of England</a> near the town in 1242.</p></div>
